data: {"id":"evt_1882b8d070019uthwTY2SYfaYs","created":1793285917959,"type":"session.reasoning.ended","location":{"directory":"C:\\work\\proj"},"data":{"sessionID":"ses_e77d48ac6ffepeEaFg961h1aSO","assistantMessageID":"msg_1882b755f001mx9DxFIoc87Evz","ordinal":0,"text":"The user wants a 2000-word essay about the history of the bicycle, no tools. I should just write it. Very detailed, roughly 2000 words. Let me write a well-structured essay with sections, covering: precursor (draisine 1817), vélocipède, penny-farthing, safety bicycle, pneumatic tires, derailleur, modern developments, e-bikes, cultural impact.\n\nAim ~2000 words. Let me write substantial prose. I'll include headings but keep it essay-like. Let me count roughly — I'll aim for about 2000 words, maybe 1900-2100.\n\nLet me draft carefully with detail and accuracy.\n\nKey facts:\n- 1817 Karl Drais, Mannheim, \"Laufmaschine\" (draisine), June 12 1817 first reported ride, ~14 km in under an hour. Patented 1818 in Baden, and in France and Britain.\n- 1818 Denis Johnson in London improved version, \"pedestrian curricle,\" hundreds of riding schools; banned on pavements; faded by 1820s.\n- 1860s: Pierre Michaux and Pierre Lallement, \"vélocipède\" with pedals on front wheel, ~1863. Lallement patent US 1866. Oliver Brothers in US. Also Pierre Michaux's claim disputed; Ernest Michaux. \n- 1868 Paris, first races; James Moore won Paris-Rouen 1869 on a Michaux, ~123 km in 10h40.\n- 1869 Edward Cowper, Thomas Bayliss, etc. British.\n- 1869: ice skating rink riding.\n- 1870s: penny-farthing /","native":{"reasoningField":"reasoning_content"}},"durable":{"aggregateID":"ses_e77d48ac6ffepeEaFg961h1aSO","seq":9,"version":1}}

